// Generated by tools/build_site.py from content/free-questions — free practice questions.
(window.FREE_Q = window.FREE_Q || {})["ii-b"] = {"qs": [["Introduction to Securities", "What is the main role of a clearing corporation in the stock market?", ["Listing new companies after scrutiny of their offer documents", "Acting as counterparty to trades and guaranteeing settlement", "Holding shareholders' securities in electronic form", "Setting the price band of each listed share"], 1, "A clearing corporation clears and settles trades, often becoming the counterparty to both buyer and seller through novation. This guarantees settlement and removes counterparty risk. Holding securities electronically is the depository's job."], ["Non-Financial Transactions", "An investor takes a loan against her mutual fund units. What does the RTA record in her folio?", ["A lien or pledge in favour of the lender", "A transfer of the units to the lender's folio", "A redemption of the units in favour of the lender", "A change of the investor's name to the lender's name"], 0, "When units are pledged, the RTA marks a lien in favour of the lender. The investor remains the owner, but cannot redeem or switch the pledged units until the lender releases the lien. If she defaults, the lender may invoke the pledge."], ["Registrar and Transfer Agents Regulations", "An RTA repeatedly fails to resolve investor complaints and ignores SEBI's directions. What action can SEBI take under the regulations?", ["Transfer the RTA's shares to AMFI", "Order the AMCs to merge with the RTA", "Only issue an advisory without any penalty", "Suspend or cancel the RTA's registration after due process"], 3, "SEBI can take action against an RTA that violates regulations, including monetary penalties, suspension or cancellation of its certificate of registration, after following the procedure in the Intermediaries Regulations. It cannot transfer ownership to AMFI."], ["Introduction to Securities", "Commercial paper is best described as:", ["A long-term secured bond issued by the central government for infrastructure", "A deposit receipt issued by banks against fixed deposits", "A share certificate issued by a company in physical form", "An unsecured short-term promissory note issued by companies"], 3, "Commercial paper is an unsecured money market instrument issued by highly rated companies to meet short-term funding needs, usually at a discount. Certificates of deposit are issued by banks, and government bonds are long-term instruments."], ["Characteristics of Debt Securities", "A zero coupon bond is one that:", ["Is issued at a discount and pays face value at maturity", "Pays interest monthly at a floating rate", "Carries no credit risk at all, as such bonds are issued only by the government", "Is repaid in equal instalments over its life"], 0, "A zero coupon bond pays no periodic interest. It is issued at a discount to its face value and the investor earns the difference when it is redeemed at face value on maturity. Its issuer can be any borrower, so credit risk may exist."], ["Banking Operations in Mutual Funds", "Redemption proceeds of a mutual fund folio are paid to:", ["Any bank account mentioned in the redemption form", "The distributor's account for onward transfer", "The bank account registered in the folio", "The account of the nominee by default"], 2, "To prevent fraud, redemption and IDCW proceeds are paid only to a bank account registered in the folio. A new account must first be registered through a proper change of bank mandate process. Distributors and nominees cannot receive proceeds."], ["Mutual Fund Products", "A multi cap fund must invest a minimum of 25% of its total assets in each of:", ["Large cap, mid cap and small cap stocks", "Equity, debt and gold", "Indian equity, foreign equity and debt", "Large cap and mid cap stocks only"], 0, "SEBI's rules require multi cap funds to invest at least 75% in equity, with at least 25% each in large, mid and small cap stocks. This differs from flexi cap funds, which can allocate freely across market capitalisations."], ["Operational Concepts of Mutual Funds", "During a new fund offer, units of an open-ended scheme are usually allotted at:", ["The NAV of an existing scheme of the same AMC", "A price decided by bidding among investors", "The face value of the units, commonly ₹10", "A premium of 10% to the face value"], 2, "In an NFO, units are generally offered at face value, typically ₹10, because the scheme has no portfolio yet. After the scheme reopens for continuous transactions, purchases and redemptions take place at the prevailing NAV."], ["Investors in Mutual Funds", "FATCA and CRS self-certification is collected from mutual fund investors mainly to:", ["Verify their income for suitability of schemes", "Check whether they hold a demat account", "Calculate stamp duty on their purchases", "Identify their tax residency for reporting accounts of foreign tax residents"], 3, "Under FATCA and the Common Reporting Standard, financial institutions in India must identify accounts of foreign tax residents and report them to tax authorities. Investors therefore declare their country of tax residence, citizenship and related details."], ["Registrar and Transfer Agents Regulations", "An RTA appoints a compliance officer. This officer is mainly responsible for:", ["Managing the investment portfolio of the mutual fund schemes serviced by the RTA", "Monitoring compliance with SEBI rules and redressal of investor grievances", "Approving the RTA's annual budget and salaries", "Marketing the RTA's services to new AMC clients"], 1, "The compliance officer monitors the RTA's compliance with the SEBI Act, regulations and circulars, reports any non-compliance and oversees the redressal of investor grievances. Portfolio management belongs to the AMC, and budgets and marketing are business functions."], ["Mutual Fund Structure and Constituents", "The trust deed of a mutual fund is executed:", ["By the AMC in favour of SEBI", "By the custodian in favour of the AMC", "By unitholders in favour of the sponsor", "By the sponsor in favour of the trustees"], 3, "The sponsor creates the mutual fund trust by executing a trust deed in favour of the trustees, who then hold the trust property for unitholders. The trust deed is registered and must be approved under SEBI's regulations."], ["Characteristics of Equity Shares", "Which statement about returns on equity shares is correct?", ["Dividends are fixed at the time of issue and paid every year", "Returns come from dividends and price changes, neither of which is guaranteed", "Capital appreciation is assured if the shares are held for at least five continuous years", "Returns are protected by the depository against market losses"], 1, "Equity returns consist of dividends, which are paid at the board's discretion, and capital gains or losses from price movements. Neither is assured, which makes equity a risk investment. Depositories hold shares but do not protect their value."], ["Basics of Mutual Funds", "Which of the following is a limitation of investing through mutual funds?", ["Investors cannot redeem units from open-ended schemes", "Investors cannot see the scheme's portfolio holdings", "Investors cannot choose the individual securities held in the portfolio", "Investors cannot invest small amounts through SIPs"], 2, "In a mutual fund, the fund manager decides the portfolio within the scheme's objective, so investors cannot customise holdings. Open-ended schemes are liquid, portfolios are disclosed regularly, and SIPs allow small investments."], ["Investors in Mutual Funds", "A Hindu Undivided Family wishes to invest in a mutual fund. The investment is made:", ["In the name of each coparcener separately", "Only through a registered trust of the family", "In the name of the eldest son as guardian", "In the name of the HUF, through its Karta"], 3, "An HUF can invest in mutual funds, with the Karta signing and operating the investment on its behalf. KYC is done for the HUF and the Karta. There is no need to form a trust or invest separately for each coparcener."], ["Financial Transactions", "An investor facing a temporary cash crunch wants to skip a few SIP instalments without cancelling the SIP. Which facility can she use, where the AMC offers it?", ["Systematic transfer plan", "Switch to IDCW option", "SIP pause", "Change of nominee"], 2, "Many AMCs offer an SIP pause facility, allowing investors to suspend instalments for a specified number of months, after which the SIP resumes automatically. STP and IDCW options serve different purposes, and nomination has no effect on SIP debits."], ["Characteristics of Other Securities", "An Indian company issues shares abroad in the form of instruments listed on a US stock exchange. These instruments are called:", ["American Depository Receipts", "Indian Depository Receipts", "Foreign Currency Convertible Bonds", "Global Treasury Receipts"], 0, "Depository receipts represent shares of a company held with a custodian and are listed abroad. When listed in the US they are called American Depository Receipts. Indian Depository Receipts are issued in India by foreign companies."], ["Characteristics of Equity Shares", "A share trades at ₹150 and the company's earnings per share is ₹5. What is its price-to-earnings (P/E) ratio?", ["7.5", "3.3", "30", "750"], 2, "The P/E ratio is market price divided by earnings per share. Here ₹150 ÷ ₹5 = 30, meaning investors pay ₹30 for every rupee of annual earnings. Multiplying the two, or dividing the other way round, gives the wrong figures."], ["Basics of Registrars and Transfer Agents", "Which RTA activity matches money received in the scheme's collection bank account with the transactions submitted by investors?", ["Portfolio rebalancing", "NAV computation", "Funds reconciliation", "Risk-o-meter updation"], 2, "Funds reconciliation compares credits in the collection account with application data so that units are allotted only against realised money. Mismatches are investigated. Rebalancing, NAV computation and risk labelling are not reconciliation activities."], ["Mutual Fund Products", "A focused fund is an equity scheme that invests in:", ["Only one sector of the economy", "Only stocks outside India", "Stocks that pay high dividends", "A maximum of 30 stocks"], 3, "SEBI defines a focused fund as one that holds a concentrated portfolio of at most 30 stocks. A single-sector scheme is a sectoral fund, overseas exposure belongs to international funds, and high-dividend stocks define dividend yield funds."], ["Mutual Fund Structure and Constituents", "SEBI requires a portion of the salary of key employees of an AMC to be paid in units of the schemes they manage or oversee. The main aim is to:", ["Increase the assets under management of newly launched schemes of the AMC", "Align the interests of these employees with those of unitholders", "Reduce the tax liability of the AMC's employees", "Replace the sponsor's contribution to the AMC"], 1, "Under the 'skin in the game' rules, a part of the compensation of designated employees is paid in scheme units with a lock-in. They gain or lose along with investors, encouraging responsible decisions. It is not aimed at raising AUM or saving tax."], ["Banking Operations in Mutual Funds", "An individual investor may register more than one bank account in a folio. Up to how many accounts are allowed?", ["5", "2", "3", "10"], 0, "Individuals and HUFs can register up to five bank accounts in a folio, while non-individual investors can register up to ten. One account is marked as default for payouts, and the others can be used for payments or chosen for redemption."], ["Investors in Mutual Funds", "A private limited company wants to invest its surplus funds in a liquid fund. Which document is typically required along with KYC?", ["A no-objection certificate from SEBI", "An approval letter from the company's bankers", "A board resolution authorising the investment and signatories", "A certificate from the registrar of companies for each investment"], 2, "Non-individual investors such as companies must submit a board resolution authorising the investment and naming authorised signatories, along with entity KYC. No SEBI, bank or ROC approval is needed for each investment."], ["Mutual Fund Products", "According to SEBI's definition, large cap companies are:", ["The 1st to 50th companies by full market capitalisation", "Companies with a market cap above ₹10,000 crore", "The 1st to 250th companies by full market capitalisation", "The 1st to 100th companies by full market capitalisation"], 3, "SEBI defines large cap companies as the top 100 companies by full market capitalisation, mid caps as the 101st to 250th, and small caps as the 251st onwards. AMFI publishes the list periodically so that schemes follow a uniform definition."], ["SEBI – Role and Regulations", "An investor's complaint against an RTA was closed on SCORES, but she is still not satisfied. Under SEBI's framework, she can next:", ["File a case directly with the Securities Appellate Tribunal", "Ask the stock exchange to cancel the RTA's registration", "Complain to the RBI banking ombudsman", "Take the dispute to the online dispute resolution platform"], 3, "SEBI's framework lets investors who are not satisfied with the resolution through the intermediary or SCORES take the dispute to the online dispute resolution (ODR) platform, which uses conciliation and online arbitration. SAT hears appeals against SEBI orders, not such disputes."], ["Basics of Mutual Funds", "An interval scheme is one that:", ["Is open for purchase and redemption only during specified intervals", "Pays IDCW at fixed intervals of one month", "Invests only in securities with fixed maturity intervals", "Switches automatically between equity and debt"], 0, "An interval scheme combines features of open and close-ended schemes. It is open for purchases and redemptions only during specified transaction periods and is otherwise closed. It must also be listed on an exchange."], ["Registrar and Transfer Agents Regulations", "Common requirements for registration and the 'fit and proper person' criteria applicable to RTAs are laid down in:", ["SEBI (Intermediaries) Regulations, 2008", "SEBI (Mutual Funds) Regulations, 1996", "SEBI (Depositories and Participants) Regulations, 2018", "Indian Trusts Act, 1882"], 0, "The SEBI (Intermediaries) Regulations, 2008 contain common provisions for all intermediaries, including RTAs, such as the fit and proper criteria and the procedure for action in case of default. The other laws govern mutual funds, depositories and trusts."], ["Introduction to Securities", "Dated government securities of the Government of India are issued in the primary market through auctions conducted by:", ["The Reserve Bank of India", "SEBI", "The National Stock Exchange", "The Ministry of Corporate Affairs"], 0, "The RBI manages the public debt of the Government of India and conducts auctions for dated securities and treasury bills. SEBI regulates the securities market but does not issue or auction government securities."], ["Investors in Mutual Funds", "Small investors who do not have a PAN, such as for micro SIPs within a specified annual limit, can invest after completing:", ["Exemption granted by SEBI on a case-by-case basis", "PAN-exempt KYC, with an alternate identity document", "A declaration from the distributor about their income", "No KYC at all, as the amounts are small"], 1, "Investments within the specified limit, such as micro SIPs, are exempt from the PAN requirement but not from KYC. Investors complete PAN-exempt KYC using another officially valid identity document, and receive a reference number."], ["Operational Concepts of Mutual Funds", "How are the recurring expenses of a scheme reflected for investors?", ["They are billed to investors separately every quarter", "They are deducted only from redemption proceeds", "They are charged once a year on the anniversary of investment", "They are accrued daily and deducted while computing the NAV"], 3, "Recurring expenses within the TER limit are charged to the scheme and accrued every day in the NAV. Investors therefore do not receive separate bills; the published NAV is already net of expenses. Exit load, if any, is deducted on redemption."], ["Mutual Fund Structure and Constituents", "The structure of a mutual fund in India is commonly described as three-tier. The three tiers are:", ["Sponsor, trustees and asset management company", "SEBI, AMFI and the asset management company", "Custodian, RTA and distributor", "Sponsor, stock exchange and depository"], 0, "The sponsor establishes the mutual fund trust, the trustees hold its property for the benefit of unitholders and supervise, and the AMC manages the schemes. SEBI and AMFI are regulator and industry body, and custodians and RTAs are service providers."], ["Characteristics of Other Securities", "A company could not pay the dividend on its cumulative preference shares for two years. What happens to this unpaid dividend?", ["It lapses and is not payable in later years", "It is converted into additional equity shares", "It is paid to the shareholders out of the Investor Education and Protection Fund", "It accumulates and must be paid before any equity dividend"], 3, "For cumulative preference shares, unpaid dividends accumulate as arrears. The company must clear these arrears before it can declare any dividend to equity shareholders. For non-cumulative preference shares, a missed dividend lapses."], ["Characteristics of Other Securities", "Preference shareholders have priority over equity shareholders in:", ["Voting on all company resolutions", "Payment of dividend and repayment of capital", "Receiving bonus shares and rights issues", "Appointing the company's directors"], 1, "Preference shares get preference over equity in receiving dividends, usually at a fixed rate, and in repayment of capital on winding up. They normally do not carry voting rights on all matters, so equity holders control the company."], ["Characteristics of Other Securities", "An investor buys a call option on a stock. Which statement correctly describes her position?", ["She has the right, but not the obligation, to buy the stock", "She is obliged to buy the stock on the expiry date", "She has the right to sell the stock to the option writer at the strike price", "She receives the premium from the option seller"], 0, "A call option buyer pays a premium for the right, but not the obligation, to buy the underlying at the strike price. The seller receives the premium and has the obligation. The right to sell belongs to a put option buyer."], ["Mutual Fund Structure and Constituents", "The agreement under which the trustees appoint the AMC to manage the mutual fund's schemes is called the:", ["Custodial services agreement", "Investment management agreement", "Memorandum of association", "Distribution agreement"], 1, "Trustees enter into an investment management agreement with the AMC, which sets out the AMC's duties in managing the schemes. Custodial and distribution agreements cover other service providers, and the memorandum relates to company incorporation."], ["Characteristics of Equity Shares", "A company splits its shares of face value ₹10 into shares of face value ₹2. An investor holding 100 shares before the split will hold:", ["500 shares of ₹2 each", "100 shares of ₹2 each", "20 shares of ₹10 each", "200 shares of ₹5 each"], 0, "A stock split divides each share into smaller units. Splitting ₹10 into ₹2 gives five shares for each share held, so 100 shares become 500 shares of ₹2 each. The total face value of the holding stays at ₹1,000."], ["Characteristics of Debt Securities", "A bond with a face value of ₹100 carries an 8% annual coupon and is currently trading at ₹80. What is its current yield?", ["8%", "10%", "6.4%", "12.5%"], 1, "Current yield is annual coupon divided by market price. The coupon is ₹8 (8% of ₹100), and ₹8 ÷ ₹80 = 10%. The coupon rate of 8% is based on face value, not on the price the investor actually pays."], ["Mutual Fund Products", "A parent invests in a children's fund, which is a solution-oriented scheme. What lock-in applies to such investments?", ["3 years from the date of each investment", "Till the child attains majority, with no other minimum lock-in period applying", "5 years or till the child attains majority, whichever is earlier", "No lock-in, as it is an open-ended scheme"], 2, "SEBI requires children's funds to have a lock-in of at least five years or until the child attains majority, whichever is earlier. Retirement funds have a similar rule linked to retirement age. A three-year lock-in applies to ELSS."], ["Characteristics of Debt Securities", "A bond with a call option allows:", ["The issuer to redeem the bond before its maturity date", "The investor to sell the bond back to the issuer early", "The investor to convert the bond into equity shares", "The issuer to stop paying interest for one year"], 0, "A call option gives the issuer the right to repay the bond early, usually when interest rates fall. The investor's right to demand early repayment is a put option. Conversion into shares is a feature of convertible bonds."], ["Basics of Mutual Funds", "Two plans of the same scheme hold the same portfolio. Why does the direct plan usually have a higher NAV than the regular plan over time?", ["It invests in higher-return securities that the regular plan is not permitted to hold", "It has a lower expense ratio as no distributor commission is paid", "It is exempt from tax on capital gains and dividends", "It is managed by a more experienced fund manager"], 1, "Direct and regular plans share one portfolio and one fund manager. The direct plan carries no distribution commission, so its expense ratio is lower and less is deducted from its NAV, which therefore grows faster over time."], ["Basics of Mutual Funds", "On a given day a debt fund reports net assets of ₹245 crore, calculated after deducting ₹5 crore of payables from its ₹250 crore holdings. If 10 crore units are in issue, the NAV works out to:", ["₹25.00", "₹24.50", "₹25.50", "₹24.00"], 1, "NAV per unit equals net assets divided by units outstanding. Net assets are ₹250 crore minus ₹5 crore, which is ₹245 crore, and ₹245 crore ÷ 10 crore units gives ₹24.50. Ignoring or adding the liabilities gives the wrong values."], ["Characteristics of Other Securities", "A warrant issued by a company gives the holder:", ["An obligation to sell shares to the company at maturity", "A fixed interest income for the life of the warrant", "The right to buy the company's shares at a set price in future", "Ownership of shares from the date of issue itself"], 2, "A warrant entitles the holder to subscribe to the issuer's shares at a predetermined price within a specified period. The holder is not obliged to exercise it and does not own the shares until exercise. Warrants carry no interest."], ["Characteristics of Other Securities", "In a futures contract on a stock, which of the following is true?", ["Only the buyer has an obligation, the seller has a right", "The buyer pays a non-refundable premium to the seller", "Neither party has any obligation until the contract expires", "Both buyer and seller are obliged to complete the contract"], 3, "A futures contract is a binding agreement in which both parties are obliged to buy or sell the underlying at the agreed price on expiry. There is no premium as in options; instead both parties deposit margins with the exchange."], ["Basics of Mutual Funds", "The total expense ratio (TER) of a scheme refers to:", ["The exit load as a percentage of redemption value", "The stamp duty as a percentage of purchase amount", "The fund manager's salary expressed as a share of the AMC's annual profit after tax", "Annual scheme expenses as a percentage of its average net assets"], 3, "TER expresses the scheme's recurring expenses, such as management fees and distribution costs, as an annual percentage of average net assets. It is accrued daily in the NAV. Exit load and stamp duty are transaction charges, not part of TER."], ["Financial Transactions", "An investor sets up an SIP with a 'top-up' feature. This means:", ["Additional units are allotted free when NAV falls", "The SIP continues even after the bank mandate expires", "Units are bought only when the market is rising", "The SIP amount increases periodically by a specified amount or percentage"], 3, "An SIP top-up, also called step-up, increases the instalment amount at chosen intervals, usually yearly, by a fixed amount or percentage. It helps investments keep pace with rising income. It does not give free units or depend on market direction."], ["Non-Financial Transactions", "A woman investor changes her surname after marriage and wants it updated in her folio. What is normally required?", ["A request with proof of name change, such as a marriage certificate, and updated KYC", "Only a phone call to the RTA's call centre", "A new PAN card in the name of her spouse", "Redemption of units and fresh purchase in the new name"], 0, "Change of name requires a written request with supporting proof such as a marriage certificate or gazette notification, and the KYC record must also be updated. A phone call is not enough, and there is no need to redeem and reinvest."], ["SEBI – Role and Regulations", "Which of the following best describes the Association of Mutual Funds in India (AMFI)?", ["The statutory regulator of mutual funds under the SEBI Act", "An industry body of SEBI-registered AMCs that promotes ethical practices", "A government department under the Finance Ministry that approves new mutual fund schemes", "A depository that holds mutual fund units in electronic form"], 1, "AMFI is an association of SEBI-registered asset management companies. It promotes professional and ethical standards, registers distributors through ARN and runs investor awareness programmes. SEBI, not AMFI, is the regulator that approves and regulates schemes."], ["Financial Transactions", "An investor bought units in a scheme on several dates and now redeems part of her holding. For computing exit load, units are generally treated as redeemed:", ["On a last-in-first-out basis", "In the order chosen by the distributor", "On a first-in-first-out basis", "At the average purchase date of all units"], 2, "Mutual funds apply the first-in-first-out (FIFO) method, so the oldest units are redeemed first. Exit load applies only to those redeemed units that fall within the load period. The same principle is used for capital gains holding periods."], ["Basics of Registrars and Transfer Agents", "An investor already has a folio with an AMC and wants to invest in another scheme of the same AMC. Which statement is correct?", ["A new folio must be opened with fresh KYC", "The new investment can be made under the same folio", "The investor must close the existing folio first", "Folios are linked to schemes, so one per scheme is required"], 1, "A folio is an investor account with an AMC and can hold units of several schemes of that AMC. Investing under the existing folio keeps holdings consolidated and avoids repeating documentation, though a new folio is also allowed if the investor wishes."], ["Basics of Mutual Funds", "A scheme's NAV is ₹20 just before it pays an IDCW of ₹1 per unit. Assuming no other change, the NAV immediately after the payout will be about:", ["₹19", "₹20", "₹21", "₹18"], 0, "An IDCW is paid out of the scheme's assets, so the NAV falls by the amount distributed. ₹20 minus ₹1 leaves about ₹19. The investor's total wealth is unchanged before tax, as the payout simply moves value from the NAV to her bank account."], ["Mutual Fund Products", "An investor wants to benefit from price differences between the cash and futures markets with low risk. Which category suits this strategy?", ["Small cap fund", "Arbitrage fund", "Credit risk fund", "Sectoral fund"], 1, "Arbitrage funds buy a stock in the cash market and sell its futures at a higher price, earning the spread with limited market risk. Small cap, credit risk and sectoral funds take higher equity or credit risk and do not follow this strategy."], ["Introduction to Securities", "What is the standard rolling settlement cycle for trades in the equity cash segment in India?", ["T+2", "T+3", "T+1", "T+5"], 2, "India moved fully to a T+1 rolling settlement cycle for the equity cash segment in January 2023, so trades settle one working day after the trade date. T+2 was the earlier cycle and is now outdated."], ["Characteristics of Other Securities", "An investor buys a put option on an index with a strike price of 22,000. In which situation will the option be profitable on expiry, ignoring the premium?", ["If the index closes above 22,000", "If the index closes below 22,000", "If the index closes exactly at 22,000", "Whatever the level at which the index closes"], 1, "A put option gives the right to sell at the strike price. It has value on expiry only if the index is below the strike, because the holder can sell at 22,000 something worth less. Above the strike, the put expires worthless."], ["Banking Operations in Mutual Funds", "An investor wants SIP instalments to be debited automatically from her bank account each month. What should be registered?", ["A demand draft for each instalment", "A NACH mandate with her bank", "A standing order with the RTA's bank", "A power of attorney in favour of the AMC"], 1, "A NACH mandate authorises the mutual fund to debit the investor's bank account for SIP instalments up to the approved amount. It is registered with the investor's bank. Demand drafts or powers of attorney are not needed for SIPs."], ["Basics of Registrars and Transfer Agents", "Applications received at an official point of acceptance are time-stamped mainly to:", ["Calculate the trail commission payable to the distributor each month", "Determine the applicable NAV for the transaction", "Fix the date for the next IDCW payout", "Decide the investor's risk profile"], 1, "Time-stamping records the exact date and time an application is received. Since the applicable NAV depends on cut-off times, this record decides which day's NAV applies. It also provides an audit trail that protects investors from backdating."], ["Characteristics of Equity Shares", "In a rights issue, new shares are offered:", ["Free of cost to all existing shareholders", "Only to qualified institutional investors at a discount to the market price", "To employees as part of their compensation", "To existing shareholders in proportion to their holdings"], 3, "A rights issue gives existing shareholders the right to buy additional shares, usually at a price below market, in proportion to what they already hold. Shares given free are bonus shares, and shares for employees come under ESOP schemes."], ["Operational Concepts of Mutual Funds", "For a purchase in an equity scheme, where the funds are available for use before the cut-off, what is the cut-off time to get the same day's NAV?", ["3:00 p.m.", "1:30 p.m.", "12:00 noon", "5:00 p.m."], 0, "For equity and most debt schemes other than liquid and overnight funds, the cut-off is 3:00 p.m. If the application is received and funds are realised before then, the same day's NAV applies. The 1:30 p.m. cut-off applies to liquid and overnight funds."], ["Mutual Fund Products", "A scheme that invests mainly in units of other mutual fund schemes is called a:", ["Fund of funds", "Balanced advantage fund", "Exchange traded fund", "Multi asset allocation fund"], 0, "A fund of funds invests in the units of other mutual fund schemes, domestic or overseas, rather than directly in securities. Investors bear expenses of both layers. ETFs and balanced advantage or multi asset funds invest directly in securities."], ["Non-Financial Transactions", "Which of the following investors cannot make a nomination in a mutual fund folio?", ["A resident individual holding singly", "A joint holder in an individual folio", "A private limited company", "A parent investing in their own name"], 2, "Nomination is available only to individuals holding units on their own behalf, singly or jointly. Non-individuals such as companies, trusts, societies and the Karta of an HUF cannot nominate, as the entity's holding passes as per its own legal arrangements."], ["Characteristics of Debt Securities", "Among the following long-term credit ratings, which one indicates the highest degree of safety for timely servicing of debt?", ["AA+", "A", "AAA", "BBB-"], 2, "Rating agencies use AAA for the highest safety and lowest credit risk. AA and A indicate high and adequate safety respectively, while BBB- is the lowest investment-grade rating. Ratings below BBB- are speculative grade."], ["SEBI – Role and Regulations", "SEBI regulations aimed at preventing persons from dealing in securities on the basis of unpublished price-sensitive information relate to:", ["Insider trading", "Takeovers", "Delisting", "Buyback of securities"], 0, "The SEBI (Prohibition of Insider Trading) Regulations prohibit trading while in possession of unpublished price-sensitive information. Takeover, delisting and buyback regulations deal with acquisitions, removing shares from exchanges and companies purchasing their own shares."], ["Financial Transactions", "If an AMC fails to transfer redemption proceeds within the time allowed by SEBI, it must pay the investor interest at the rate of:", ["6% per annum for the delay", "15% per annum for the delay", "The bank's savings account rate", "No interest, only an apology letter"], 1, "SEBI regulations require the AMC to pay interest at 15% per annum for the period of delay beyond the stipulated time for transfer of redemption or repurchase proceeds. This interest is borne by the AMC, not the scheme."], ["Investors in Mutual Funds", "An NRI invests in a mutual fund using money from her NRO account. How is this investment treated?", ["On a fully repatriable basis", "As a foreign portfolio investment", "On a non-repatriable basis", "As a deposit under FEMA"], 2, "Money from an NRO account represents income earned in India, so investments from it are generally non-repatriable, with limited repatriation allowed under RBI rules. Investments from NRE or FCNR accounts are repatriable."], ["Non-Financial Transactions", "After the death of a sole unitholder, the AMC transmits the units to the registered nominee. What is the effect for the AMC?", ["It must hold the units until all legal heirs give consent", "It must pay the units' value to the legal heirs as well", "It must redeem the units and deposit the money in court until the heirs settle their claims", "It is discharged of its liability for those units towards the legal heirs"], 3, "Transmission to a valid registered nominee gives the AMC a valid discharge. Any claim that the legal heirs may have is a matter between them and the nominee under succession law, and does not require the AMC to pay twice or hold the units."], ["Investors in Mutual Funds", "Who can act as guardian for a minor's mutual fund folio?", ["Any adult relative nominated by the minor", "A natural guardian (parent) or a court-appointed legal guardian", "The distributor who sources the investment", "Any person who funds the investment"], 1, "SEBI's norms allow either a natural guardian, that is, a parent, or a court-appointed legal guardian to operate a minor's folio. Proof of the relationship or the court order must be provided. Relatives or distributors cannot act as guardian merely by funding the investment."], ["Basics of Mutual Funds", "In an open-ended scheme, investors can:", ["Buy units only during the new fund offer", "Redeem units only on the scheme's maturity date", "Buy and redeem units from the fund on an ongoing basis", "Trade units only with other investors on stock exchanges during market hours"], 2, "Open-ended schemes issue and redeem units on a continuous basis at NAV-based prices, so there is no fixed maturity. Close-ended schemes accept money only during the NFO and are listed for liquidity until maturity."], ["Banking Operations in Mutual Funds", "Large redemption payouts to investors are usually credited directly through:", ["Cash payments at the RTA's service centre", "Demand drafts sent by ordinary post", "Electronic modes such as NEFT, RTGS or IMPS", "Postal money orders to the registered address"], 2, "Mutual funds credit redemption and IDCW proceeds electronically to the registered bank account through NEFT, RTGS or IMPS, which is faster and safer. Cheques or drafts are used only where electronic credit is not possible, and cash is never paid."], ["Operational Concepts of Mutual Funds", "An investor submits a redemption request for an equity scheme at 2:30 p.m. on a business day. Which NAV applies?", ["The NAV of the previous business day", "The NAV of the next business day", "The NAV of the same business day", "The average NAV of the week"], 2, "For redemptions in equity schemes, requests received before the 3:00 p.m. cut-off get the same day's closing NAV. Unlike purchases, redemption does not depend on realisation of funds, so time of receipt alone decides the NAV."], ["Non-Financial Transactions", "Why do RTAs verify a new email address or mobile number before updating it in a folio?", ["Because SEBI charges a fee for each update", "Because distributors earn an extra commission whenever an investor's contact details change", "Because transaction alerts go there, and fraudulent changes could hide misuse", "Because the NAV depends on the investor's location"], 2, "Contact details receive OTPs, alerts and statements. If a fraudster changes them, the real investor may not learn of unauthorised transactions. Validation ensures the details belong to the investor. Fees, commissions and NAV are unrelated."], ["Characteristics of Other Securities", "A Real Estate Investment Trust (REIT) in India mainly invests in:", ["Housing loans given to individual home buyers", "Shares of listed real estate developers only", "Completed, income-generating real estate such as office parks", "Undeveloped agricultural land, to be sold later when land prices have risen"], 2, "REITs pool money from investors to own completed, rent-yielding properties, mainly commercial office space, and distribute most of their income. They are regulated by SEBI. They do not lend to home buyers or mainly hold developer shares."], ["Financial Transactions", "An investor holds 1,000 units under the IDCW reinvestment option. The scheme declares ₹2 per unit and the ex-IDCW NAV is ₹20. Ignoring tax deduction, how many additional units will be allotted?", ["200", "50", "2,000", "100"], 3, "IDCW payable is 1,000 × ₹2 = ₹2,000. Under the reinvestment option this is used to buy units at the ex-IDCW NAV of ₹20, giving ₹2,000 ÷ ₹20 = 100 units. In practice, TDS may reduce the amount reinvested."], ["Registrar and Transfer Agents Regulations", "Which of the following is expected of an RTA under its code of conduct?", ["Exercising due diligence and not misrepresenting facts to investors or clients", "Giving preference to large investors when processing requests", "Recommending schemes of AMCs that pay the RTA higher fees", "Sharing investor data with group companies so that they can cross-sell other products"], 0, "The code of conduct requires an RTA to act with integrity, due diligence and fairness, treat all investors equally and keep information confidential. Favouring large investors, product pushing or sharing data for marketing would breach the code."], ["Financial Transactions", "An investor redeems 300 units at an NAV of ₹45. A 1% exit load applies. What amount will she receive, ignoring taxes?", ["₹13,365", "₹13,500", "₹13,455", "₹13,635"], 0, "Redemption value is 300 × ₹45 = ₹13,500. A 1% exit load is ₹135, so the investor receives ₹13,365. Adding the load instead of deducting it, or ignoring it altogether, gives the incorrect values."], ["Investors in Mutual Funds", "An investor completed KYC through one AMC. When she invests with a different AMC, what happens?", ["Her KYC record with the KRA can be used, so full KYC need not be repeated", "She must complete fresh in-person verification with the new AMC before her first investment", "She must obtain a KYC transfer letter from SEBI", "She can invest only in the same category of schemes"], 0, "KYC details are uploaded to a KYC registration agency, and other SEBI-registered intermediaries can access and rely on that record. This avoids repeating KYC. Her KYC should be in a validated status for smooth processing."], ["Mutual Fund Structure and Constituents", "A mutual fund wants an independent entity to verify that its scheme accounts are fairly stated. It must appoint:", ["The same auditor as the sponsor company", "The custodian to certify the accounts", "An auditor independent of the auditor of the AMC", "The RTA's internal audit team"], 2, "Scheme accounts must be audited by an independent auditor, who cannot be the auditor of the AMC. This separation strengthens objectivity. Custodians and RTAs are service providers and cannot act as statutory auditors of the schemes."], ["SEBI – Role and Regulations", "The preamble of the SEBI Act states SEBI's purpose as:", ["Fixing interest rates and controlling money supply in the economy", "Collecting taxes on capital gains from securities transactions", "Protecting investors and developing and regulating the securities market", "Guaranteeing returns to investors in listed securities"], 2, "SEBI's three core objectives are to protect the interests of investors in securities, to promote the development of the securities market and to regulate it. Monetary policy is the RBI's job, and taxation belongs to the income tax department."], ["Non-Financial Transactions", "An investor has three different folios with the same AMC with identical holders and details. She wants a single folio. This request is called:", ["Transmission of folio units", "Consolidation of folios", "Switch of units", "Change of status"], 1, "Consolidation merges multiple folios with the same holders in the same order and the same mode of holding into one folio, making records easier to manage. Transmission relates to death of a holder, and switches move money between schemes."], ["Characteristics of Debt Securities", "If market interest rates rise, what generally happens to the prices of existing fixed-coupon bonds?", ["They rise", "They remain unchanged until maturity", "They fall", "They rise only for long-term bonds"], 2, "Bond prices move inversely to interest rates. When rates rise, new bonds offer higher coupons, so older bonds with lower coupons become less attractive and their prices fall until their yield matches the market."], ["Financial Transactions", "An investor wants to redeem ₹10,000 from a scheme whose NAV is ₹25, with no exit load. How many units will be redeemed?", ["250", "400", "2,500", "4,000"], 1, "For an amount-based redemption, units redeemed equal the amount divided by NAV. ₹10,000 ÷ ₹25 = 400 units. The RTA reduces the folio balance by 400 units and pays ₹10,000 to the registered bank account."], ["Basics of Mutual Funds", "An investor bought units at an NAV of ₹10 and the NAV is now ₹12.50. Ignoring loads and IDCW, what is her absolute return?", ["2.5%", "20%", "125%", "25%"], 3, "Absolute return is (current NAV minus purchase NAV) divided by purchase NAV. Here (12.50 − 10) ÷ 10 = 0.25, or 25%. The 20% figure wrongly divides the gain by the current NAV instead of the purchase NAV."], ["Registrar and Transfer Agents Regulations", "SEBI has designated certain RTAs as Qualified RTAs (QRTAs). Such RTAs are identified on the basis of:", ["Being owned by a public sector bank", "Being listed on a stock exchange for three years", "The large number of folios they service, making them systemically important", "Having only one AMC as a client"], 2, "QRTAs are RTAs that service a very large number of folios and are therefore systemically important. SEBI places enhanced obligations on them, covering governance, risk management, systems audit and business continuity. Ownership or listing is not the criterion."], ["Introduction to Securities", "Shares held in dematerialised form by investors in India are held in electronic accounts with:", ["NSDL or CDSL, through a depository participant", "The stock exchange where the shares are listed", "The registrar of companies of the issuer's state", "The company's own bankers as safe custody"], 0, "India has two depositories, NSDL and CDSL, which hold securities in electronic form. Investors access them through depository participants such as banks and brokers. Stock exchanges provide trading platforms but do not hold investors' securities."], ["Financial Transactions", "An investor switches units from an equity scheme into a debt scheme of the same AMC within the exit load period of the equity scheme. Which statement is correct?", ["Exit load of the equity scheme may apply on the switch-out", "No load applies because the AMC is the same", "Load is charged on the switch-in to the debt scheme instead of on the switch-out", "The switch is not allowed during the load period"], 0, "A switch is a redemption from one scheme and a purchase in another. So the exit load of the source scheme can apply to units switched out within its load period, unless the scheme documents waive it. There is no entry load on the switch-in."], ["Mutual Fund Products", "A liquid fund can invest in debt and money market securities with a maturity of up to:", ["1 day", "1 year", "91 days", "3 years"], 2, "Liquid funds invest in debt and money market instruments maturing within 91 days. Overnight funds are limited to one-day maturity, and ultra short or low duration funds hold longer maturities. This short maturity keeps interest rate risk low."], ["Basics of Registrars and Transfer Agents", "A scheme declares an IDCW with a fixed record date. Which RTA function decides who is entitled to it?", ["Valuing the scheme's securities at market prices", "Placing orders with brokers for the scheme", "Preparing the scheme's annual financial statements", "Maintaining the register of unitholders as on the record date"], 3, "IDCW is paid to unitholders whose names appear in the register on the record date. The RTA maintains this register, so it identifies eligible investors and their holdings. Valuation, trading and annual accounts are AMC or fund accounting functions."], ["Characteristics of Debt Securities", "An investor receives half-yearly coupons from a bond but interest rates in the market have fallen, so the coupons can only be invested at lower rates. This is an example of:", ["Credit risk", "Reinvestment risk", "Liquidity risk", "Market liquidity risk"], 1, "Reinvestment risk is the risk that interim cash flows such as coupons must be reinvested at lower rates than expected. It is highest when rates fall. Credit risk relates to default by the issuer, and liquidity risk to difficulty in selling."], ["SEBI – Role and Regulations", "A mutual fund distributor is aggrieved by a penalty order passed by SEBI. To which body can the distributor appeal?", ["Securities Appellate Tribunal", "National Company Law Tribunal", "AMFI's disciplinary committee", "The Reserve Bank of India"], 0, "Appeals against orders of SEBI lie with the Securities Appellate Tribunal (SAT). NCLT deals with company law matters, AMFI is an industry association and the RBI is the banking regulator; none of them hears appeals against SEBI."], ["Operational Concepts of Mutual Funds", "A purchase request for a liquid fund, with funds credited in time, is received at 2:00 p.m. on a Wednesday that is a business day. Which NAV applies?", ["Wednesday's NAV", "Thursday's NAV", "Tuesday's NAV", "Friday's NAV"], 0, "For liquid funds, applications received after the 1:30 p.m. cut-off get the closing NAV of the day immediately preceding the next business day. As Thursday is the next business day, the NAV of Wednesday, the preceding day, applies."], ["Characteristics of Debt Securities", "Two bonds have the same coupon and credit rating, but one matures in 2 years and the other in 15 years. If interest rates change, which statement is correct?", ["The 2-year bond's price will change more", "Both prices will change by the same amount", "Neither price will change as the coupons are fixed", "The 15-year bond's price will change more"], 3, "Longer maturity bonds have higher duration, so their prices are more sensitive to interest rate changes. The 15-year bond will therefore show a larger price movement than the 2-year bond for the same change in rates."], ["Banking Operations in Mutual Funds", "Under SEBI's rules for mutual fund transactions, investment platforms and distributors may NOT:", ["Help investors place mutual fund orders through stock exchange platforms like BSE StAR MF", "Collect transaction orders from investors electronically", "Earn commission on investments in regular plans", "Pool investors' money in their own accounts before paying the AMC"], 3, "SEBI has prohibited pooling of investor funds by intermediaries in mutual fund transactions. Money must flow directly from the investor's bank account to the mutual fund's account, and redemptions directly back, reducing the risk of misuse."], ["Basics of Registrars and Transfer Agents", "After an RTA processes a purchase, the investor receives an SMS and email with details of units allotted. This communication is sent:", ["By the RTA on behalf of the AMC", "By SEBI after approving the allotment", "By the stock exchange where units trade", "By the investor's bank after the debit"], 0, "RTAs send transaction confirmations and account statements to investors on behalf of the AMC, using the contact details in the folio. SEBI does not approve individual allotments, and the bank only confirms the debit of money."], ["SEBI – Role and Regulations", "SEBI was first set up in 1988 and was given statutory powers through:", ["The Companies Act, 1956", "The SEBI Act, 1992", "The Securities Contracts (Regulation) Act, 1956", "The Depositories Act, 1996"], 1, "SEBI started as a non-statutory body in 1988 and became a statutory regulator when the SEBI Act was passed in 1992. The Act sets out its powers and functions. The other Acts deal with companies, contracts and depositories."], ["SEBI – Role and Regulations", "A party wants to challenge a decision of the Securities Appellate Tribunal. The next appeal lies with:", ["SEBI's whole-time members", "The Ministry of Finance", "The relevant stock exchange", "The Supreme Court of India"], 3, "Under the SEBI Act, an appeal against an order of the Securities Appellate Tribunal lies with the Supreme Court. SEBI itself cannot review SAT orders, and neither the Ministry of Finance nor stock exchanges have appellate powers here."], ["Operational Concepts of Mutual Funds", "An investor's SIP of ₹20,000 goes into a scheme with an NAV of ₹40. After stamp duty of 0.005%, how many units are allotted?", ["500.000", "499.975", "499.950", "499.995"], 1, "Stamp duty is 0.005% of ₹20,000, which is ₹1, so ₹19,999 is invested. Dividing by the NAV of ₹40 gives 499.975 units. Ignoring stamp duty would wrongly give 500 units."], ["Characteristics of Equity Shares", "What does 'limited liability' mean for an equity shareholder of a company limited by shares?", ["Dividend is limited to a fixed rate each year", "Voting rights are limited to special resolutions", "The shares can be sold only to limited buyers", "Loss is limited to the amount invested or unpaid on the shares"], 3, "Limited liability means a shareholder cannot be asked to pay the company's debts beyond the amount unpaid on their shares. Once shares are fully paid, the maximum loss is the investment itself. It has nothing to do with dividend rate or voting."], ["Mutual Fund Structure and Constituents", "Under SEBI's regulations, what is the minimum net worth an AMC must maintain?", ["₹10 crore", "₹25 crore", "₹50 crore", "₹100 crore"], 2, "SEBI requires an AMC to have and maintain a net worth of at least ₹50 crore. This was raised from the earlier ₹10 crore requirement to ensure that AMCs have adequate financial strength to run their business."], ["Investors in Mutual Funds", "A folio is held jointly by two investors with the mode of holding 'joint'. What is required to redeem units?", ["Signatures of both holders", "Signature of the first holder only", "Signature of either holder", "Signature of the nominee"], 0, "In 'joint' mode, all holders must sign for transactions such as redemption. In 'anyone or survivor' mode, any one holder can transact. Nominees have no rights while the holders are alive."], ["SEBI – Role and Regulations", "Which of these is a power available to SEBI under the SEBI Act?", ["Granting banking licences to new private banks", "Fixing the face value of shares of every company", "Conducting inspections and investigations of intermediaries", "Deciding the income tax rate on dividends"], 2, "SEBI can call for information, inspect books, investigate intermediaries such as RTAs and AMCs, and impose penalties. Banking licences are issued by the RBI, face value is decided by companies within the law, and tax rates are set by Parliament."], ["Non-Financial Transactions", "A folio is held jointly by two persons. One of them dies. The units will normally be:", ["Transferred to the nominee of the deceased", "Transmitted to the surviving joint holder", "Redeemed and paid to the legal heirs", "Frozen until a court order is received"], 1, "In a joint folio, on the death of one holder, the units go to the surviving holder or holders, who submit the death certificate and updated KYC. Nominees come into the picture only when all holders have died."], ["Characteristics of Equity Shares", "If a company is wound up, equity shareholders are paid:", ["Before secured lenders, since equity shareholders are the owners of the company", "Along with debenture holders, in equal priority", "Only after creditors and preference shareholders are paid", "Before preference shareholders but after banks"], 2, "Equity shareholders have a residual claim. On liquidation, secured and unsecured creditors are paid first, then preference shareholders, and only what remains goes to equity shareholders. This is why equity carries higher risk than debt."], ["Non-Financial Transactions", "A sole unitholder has died without registering a nominee. Who can claim the units?", ["The distributor who sold the scheme", "The AMC, which redeems and keeps the money", "Anyone who holds the original account statement and the deceased's PAN card copy", "The legal heirs, after submitting the required succession documents"], 3, "Without a nominee or joint holder, units are transmitted to the legal heirs. The RTA asks for documents such as the death certificate, proof of legal heirship and indemnity, or a succession certificate or probate where the value is high."], ["Mutual Fund Structure and Constituents", "What proportion of the directors on an AMC's board must be independent, as per SEBI's requirements?", ["At least one-third", "At least two-thirds", "At least 25%", "At least 50%"], 3, "SEBI requires that at least half of the AMC's board consist of independent directors, that is, persons not associated with the sponsor. For the board of the trustee company, the requirement is stricter at two-thirds independent trustees."], ["Operational Concepts of Mutual Funds", "Which statement about entry load in Indian mutual funds is correct?", ["Entry load of up to 2.25% is allowed on equity schemes", "SEBI has abolished entry load on all schemes", "Entry load is charged only on regular plans", "Entry load applies only to SIP instalments"], 1, "SEBI abolished entry loads in 2009, so no load can be charged at the time of purchase. Distributors are paid commission by the AMC out of scheme expenses in regular plans, and investors in direct plans pay no commission."], ["Banking Operations in Mutual Funds", "Before registering a new bank account, many RTAs credit a small amount to the account to check the name and account number. This is known as:", ["Cheque truncation", "Reverse mortgage", "Standing instruction", "Penny drop validation"], 3, "In penny drop validation, a small amount such as ₹1 is credited to the account, and the bank's response confirms that the account is active and gives the holder's name. This is matched with the investor's name before registration."], ["Basics of Registrars and Transfer Agents", "Which of these tasks is typically carried out by a mutual fund RTA on behalf of the AMC?", ["Selecting securities for the scheme's portfolio", "Approving the scheme information document", "Calculating commission payable to distributors", "Fixing the scheme's benchmark index"], 2, "RTAs compute the upfront and trail commission due to distributors based on transaction and holding data, which the AMC then pays. Portfolio selection is done by the fund manager, and approval of scheme documents involves the AMC, trustees and SEBI."], ["Introduction to Securities", "A company sells new shares to the public for the first time through an IPO. In which market does this transaction take place?", ["Secondary market", "Money market", "Over-the-counter debt market", "Primary market"], 3, "An IPO is a fresh issue of securities by the issuer to investors, so money flows to the company. That is a primary market transaction. Later trading of these shares between investors happens in the secondary market."], ["Registrar and Transfer Agents Regulations", "Under the RTA regulations, a Category II registrant may act as:", ["Both a registrar to an issue and a share transfer agent, for listed and unlisted companies", "A registrar only for government securities", "A share transfer agent only for its group companies", "Either a registrar to an issue or a share transfer agent, but not both"], 3, "Category I permits an entity to act as both registrar to an issue and share transfer agent. Category II permits it to act in only one of the two capacities. There is no category limited to government securities or group companies."], ["Basics of Registrars and Transfer Agents", "An AMC outsources its investor servicing to an RTA. If an investor faces deficient service, who remains ultimately responsible to the investor?", ["The AMC", "The RTA alone", "The investor's distributor", "AMFI"], 0, "An AMC can outsource operations to an RTA, but it cannot outsource its responsibility. The AMC remains accountable to investors for the RTA's work and must monitor service standards. The RTA is answerable to the AMC under their agreement."], ["Basics of Registrars and Transfer Agents", "MF Central, a common platform where investors can carry out service requests across mutual fund houses, has been developed jointly by:", ["SEBI and the Reserve Bank of India", "NSDL and CDSL", "AMFI and the stock exchanges", "The two large mutual fund RTAs, CAMS and KFintech"], 3, "MF Central was developed by the RTAs CAMS and KFin Technologies, under SEBI's direction, as a single platform for investors to view holdings and submit financial and non-financial transactions across AMCs they service."], ["Introduction to Securities", "A company wants to raise funds for ten years to build a new plant. Which instrument suits this need?", ["A 91-day treasury bill", "A 10-year corporate bond", "A 3-month commercial paper", "A 6-month certificate of deposit"], 1, "Long-term needs are met through capital market instruments, which usually have maturities above one year. A 10-year bond qualifies, while treasury bills, commercial paper and certificates of deposit are short-term money market instruments maturing within a year."], ["Characteristics of Equity Shares", "A shareholder holds 100 shares of a company. The company announces a 1:1 bonus issue. After the bonus, the shareholder will hold:", ["150 shares, after paying the face value", "200 shares, without paying any money", "200 shares, after paying the market price", "100 shares with double the face value"], 1, "In a 1:1 bonus, one free share is given for every share held, so 100 shares become 200. Bonus shares are issued by capitalising reserves, so the shareholder pays nothing. Total value of the holding does not change on its own."], ["Banking Operations in Mutual Funds", "Which of these is a permitted exception to the ban on third-party payments for mutual fund purchases?", ["A close friend paying for an investor's lump sum purchase through his own cheque", "An employer paying for an employee's SIP through payroll deductions", "A distributor paying on behalf of a client", "A neighbour paying through an online transfer"], 1, "Third-party payments are generally not accepted. Exceptions include payment by an employer for employees through payroll deduction, by a parent or grandparent for a minor within a limit, and by a custodian for an FPI, with required declarations."], ["Mutual Fund Products", "A gilt fund must invest at least 80% of its total assets in:", ["AAA-rated corporate bonds", "Government securities across maturities", "Bank certificates of deposit", "Treasury bills only"], 1, "Gilt funds invest a minimum of 80% in government securities across maturities. They have negligible credit risk but can face significant interest rate risk. Corporate bonds and CDs belong to other debt categories."], ["Banking Operations in Mutual Funds", "An investor wants to stop her SIP permanently and also cancel the auto-debit. What should she do?", ["Submit an SIP cancellation request and cancel the NACH mandate", "Keep the account balance low so that debits fail", "Redeem all units, as this cancels the SIP automatically", "Write to SEBI to stop the debits"], 0, "SIPs should be cancelled through a formal request to the AMC or RTA, along with cancellation of the mandate. Letting debits fail is not correct practice, as banks may charge penalties. Redeeming units does not necessarily stop future SIP instalments."], ["Characteristics of Debt Securities", "A secured debenture differs from an unsecured debenture because a secured debenture:", ["Is guaranteed by SEBI against default", "Always carries a higher coupon rate", "Can be converted into equity shares", "Is backed by a charge on specified assets of the issuer"], 3, "A secured debenture has a charge created on the issuer's assets, which holders can enforce if the issuer defaults. SEBI does not guarantee any security. Convertibility is a separate feature, and secured debt usually pays a lower coupon."], ["Mutual Fund Structure and Constituents", "Which of these is a function of the AMC rather than the trustees?", ["Making day-to-day investment decisions for the schemes", "Ensuring that the AMC acts in unitholders' interests", "Reviewing whether the AMC complies with SEBI regulations and the trust deed", "Approving the investment management agreement"], 0, "The AMC manages money, so investment decisions for the schemes are its job. The trustees play a supervisory role: they appoint the AMC, approve key agreements and check that the AMC complies with regulations and protects unitholders."], ["Registrar and Transfer Agents Regulations", "As an intermediary, an RTA has obligations under the Prevention of Money Laundering Act. Which of these is one of them?", ["Fixing the minimum investment amount for schemes", "Reporting suspicious transactions to FIU-IND", "Approving the KYC of foreign portfolio investors for SEBI", "Collecting income tax on capital gains from investors"], 1, "Under the PMLA and SEBI's AML guidelines, intermediaries including RTAs must monitor transactions, maintain records and report suspicious transactions to the Financial Intelligence Unit-India. They do not set scheme minimums or collect capital gains tax."], ["Registrar and Transfer Agents Regulations", "Before an entity can act as a registrar or share transfer agent for securities, it must:", ["Obtain a licence from the RBI", "Become a member of a stock exchange", "Obtain a certificate of registration from SEBI", "Register as a distributor with AMFI"], 2, "Acting as a registrar to an issue or share transfer agent requires a certificate of registration from SEBI under the relevant regulations. RBI licences, exchange membership and AMFI registration apply to other activities."], ["Characteristics of Equity Shares", "When a company buys back its own shares from shareholders, the usual effect is that:", ["The number of shares outstanding is reduced", "The face value of each share is increased", "New shares are issued to existing shareholders", "The company's debt is converted into equity"], 0, "In a buyback, the company uses its funds to purchase its own shares, which are then extinguished. This reduces the number of shares outstanding and can raise earnings per share. It does not change face value or issue new shares."], ["Introduction to Securities", "An investor opens a demat account through her bank to hold shares electronically. In this arrangement, the bank acts as a:", ["Clearing member", "Registrar to an issue", "Depository participant", "Custodian of the depository"], 2, "A depository participant is the agent of a depository through which investors open and operate demat accounts. Many banks and brokers are registered DPs. A clearing member settles trades on an exchange, which is a different role."], ["Operational Concepts of Mutual Funds", "The exit load collected by a scheme when investors redeem units is:", ["Kept by the AMC as its income", "Paid to the distributor as commission", "Transferred to SEBI's investor protection fund", "Credited back to the scheme, net of GST"], 3, "SEBI requires exit load, net of GST, to be credited to the scheme rather than kept by the AMC. This benefits the remaining investors who bear the cost of redemptions. Distributor commission comes from scheme expenses, not exit load."]]};
